<section xml:id="cmse381-lec20-polynomialandstepfunctions-worksheet" component="ws20">
  <title>Jupyter Notebook: Lec 20 - Polynomial Regression and Step Functions</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In this module we are going to implement polynomial regression and step functions as discussed in class.</p>
    <program language="python">
      # Everyone's favorite standard imports
      import numpy as np
      import pandas as pd
      import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

      %matplotlib inline


      # ML imports we've used previously
      # import sklearn
      from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
      from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
      from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures
    </program>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec20-polynomialandstepfunctions-worksheet-ptx-0-loading-in-the-data">
    <title>0. Loading in the data</title>
    <p>We're going to use the <c>Wage</c> data used in the book, so note that many of your plots can be checked by looking at figures in the book.</p>
    <program language="python">
      url = "https://msu-cmse-courses.github.io/CMSE381-S26/_downloads/2d664388c56e51af2d83cea1fe3027f4/Wage.csv"
      df = pd.read_csv(url, index_col =0 )
      df.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      df.info()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      df.describe()
    </program>
    <p>Here's the plot we used multiple times in class to look at a single variable: <c>age</c> vs <c>wage</c>. I've also added some splits so that the people making above and below $250,000 are drawn in a different color.</p>
    <program language="python">
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &lt;=250], df.wage[df.wage&lt;=250],marker = '*', label = '&lt; 250')
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &gt;250], df.wage[df.wage&gt;250], label = '&gt; 250')
      plt.legend()

      plt.xlabel('Age')
      plt.ylabel('Wage')
      plt.show()
    </program>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec21-polynomialandstepfunctions-worksheet-ptx-1-linear-regression">
    <title>1. Linear Regression</title>
    <p>Before we do anything fancy, let's just do some linear regression. It's not going to be a great fit to our data, this is just to see how we can draw the function learned.</p>
    <p>If I want to learn a linear model predicting <c>wage</c> from <c>age</c>, I can do the same thing we've done for the last month.</p>
    <program language="python">
      from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      X = df.age.values.reshape(-1,1)
      y = df.wage

      linreg = LinearRegression()

      linreg.fit(X,y)
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # If you edit the y_wage stuff above, you'll get the linear regression line drawn here.


      # This is all the stuff to plot the data
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &lt;=250], df.wage[df.wage&lt;=250],marker = '*', label = '&lt; 250')
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &gt;250], df.wage[df.wage&gt;250], label = '&gt; 250')
      plt.legend()

      plt.xlabel('Age')
      plt.ylabel('Wage')

      # This is what I'm adding to draw the line 
      plt.plot(t_age, y_wage, color = 'red')
      plt.show()
    </program>
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is the equation learned by the model?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Read the slope from <c>linreg.coef_[0]</c> and the intercept from <c>linreg.intercept_</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Write the fitted model in the form <m>\widehat{wage}=\beta_0+\beta_1 age</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not reverse the intercept and slope; <c>intercept_</c> is the constant term and <c>coef_</c> multiplies age.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  print('beta_1:', round(linreg.coef_[0],3))
                  print('beta_0:', round(linreg.intercept_,3))

                  print('wage = ', round(linreg.intercept_,3), ' + ', round(linreg.coef_[0],3), '* age')
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Now I could plot this by taking the equation I just learned and applying it to some vector of values. However, it is even easier to do this by simply predicting the outputs for a lot of the <m>x</m>-inputs and then drawing the function on top of the data.</p>
              <p>Use the <c>predict</c> function to get the function outputs and then plot this using the code below.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Create a dense grid of ages and reshape it to one column before calling <c>predict</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Plot the predicted wages against the same age grid, on top of the scatter plot.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> A one-dimensional age array has the wrong shape for scikit-learn; use <c>.reshape(-1, 1)</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  t_age = np.linspace(20,80, 100).reshape(-1,1)

                  # your code here for y_wage
                  y_wage = linreg.predict(t_age)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec21-polynomialandstepfunctions-worksheet-ptx-1-polynomial-regression">
    <title>1. Polynomial Regression</title>
    <p>Our first step is to build a polynomial regression model using the age data to predict wage. So, as in class, we are in <m>p=1</m> world here where we are going to fit the model
    <md>
    \texttt{wage} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \texttt{age} + \beta_2 \texttt{age}^2 + \cdots + \beta_p \texttt{age}^p +\varepsilon.
    </md></p>
    <p>The trick here is to build a matrix <m>X</m> which has a column containing <c>age</c>, one with <c>age^2</c>, one with <c>age^3</c>, etc. Then we hand this to your favorite regression tool (it doesn't need to know it's getting polynomial matrix inputs, it just sees a matrix of features and does it's thing).</p>
    <p>Here's the code we learned in <url href="../14-Ch5_1-More_KFoldCV/index.md">Lecture 14</url> for building the data frame of powers of input <m>X</m>.</p>
    <program language="python">
      p = 3
      poly = PolynomialFeatures(p, include_bias = False)
      X_powers = poly.fit_transform(X)

      # X_powers

      model = LinearRegression()
      model.fit(X_powers,y)
      print(f"Coefs: {model.coef_}")
      print(f"Intercept: {model.intercept_}")
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # Note: this should look like slide from class.
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &lt;=250], df.wage[df.wage&lt;=250],marker = '*')
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &gt;250], df.wage[df.wage&gt;250])

      plt.xlabel('Age')
      plt.ylabel('Wage')

      plt.plot(t_age, y_wage, c= 'red')
      plt.show()
    </program>
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>What is the equation of the model learned?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>The columns of <c>X_powers</c> are <m>age</m>, <m>age^2</m>, and <m>age^3</m> in that order.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Pair <c>model.coef_[i]</c> with power <m>i+1</m> and use <c>model.intercept_</c> as the constant term.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not treat the three coefficients as three unrelated predictors; they multiply successive powers of age.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  # My cheating to print it out 
                  linestring = str(round(model.intercept_,2))
                  for i in range(p):
                      linestring += ' + ' + str(round(model.coef_[i],2))+ ' X^'+ str(i+1)
                  print( 'y = ', linestring)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>As before, in order to plot we want to determine <c>y_wage</c> from the <c>t_age</c> input below by using the <c>predict</c> command. However, note that to do this, you're going to have to pass in the polynomial matrix for the <c>t_age</c> column.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Apply the already configured <c>poly</c> transformer to <c>t_age</c> before prediction.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>The transformed grid must have the same three feature columns used to fit <c>model</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not pass raw <c>t_age</c> directly to a model trained on polynomial features; its feature count will not match.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  t_age = np.linspace(10,90,100).reshape(-1,1)

                  # Make the polynomial features from t_age 
                  t_powers = poly.fit_transform(t_age)

                  # Now predict the y values from the model and the t_powers
                  y_wage = model.predict(t_powers)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec21-polynomialandstepfunctions-worksheet-ptx-2-step-functions">
    <title>2. Step functions</title>
    <p>Now let's try to use step functions to learn a model using <c>age</c> to predict <c>wage</c>. Like with the polynomial example from last time, all we're going to do is build a data frame or feature matrix that has the step function values in each column, and then pass that matrix to our favorite linear modeling function.</p>
    <p>First, we want to get a dataframe with the cuts.</p>
    <program language="python">
      df_cut, bins = pd.cut(df.age, 4, retbins = True, right = False)
    </program>
    <p>Note that the <c>df_cut</c> is a pandas series with each data point now represented as the interval it's contained in.</p>
    <program language="python">
      df_cut
    </program>
    <p>Here I'm just printing it out in a column next to the <c>age</c> information that was used to generate it.</p>
    <program language="python">
      pd.DataFrame({'age': df.age, 'df_cut': df_cut}).head(10)
    </program>
    <p>The <c>bins</c> output gives me the <m>c_i</m> knots as follows.</p>
    <program language="python">
      print(bins)
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # This is how it matches with our notation.
      print(r'c_1 = ', bins[0])
      print(r'c_2 = ', bins[1])
      print(r'c_3 = ', bins[2])
      print(r'c_4 = ', bins[3])
      print(r'c_5 = ', bins[4])
    </program>
    <p>We can use the dummy variable trick to turn the <c>df_cut</c> output into something closer to what we are using. Below is my code that generates the data frame storing <m>C_i(X)</m> for all our entries.</p>
    <program language="python">
      df_steps_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df_cut) # This gives us entries with true/false
      df_steps = df_steps_dummies.apply(lambda x: x * 1) # This converts those to either 0 or 1.
      df_steps
    </program>
    <p>Assuming you stored your linear regression model as <c>linreg</c>, the following code will plot the learned function. Check that the answers you got in the table above match with what you're seeing in the graph.</p>
    <program language="python">
      t_age = pd.Series(np.linspace(20,80,100))
      t_df_cut = pd.cut(t_age, bins, right = False) #&lt;-- I'm explicitly passing the same bins learned above so tha the procedure is the same. 

      t_dummies = pd.get_dummies(t_df_cut)
      t_step = t_dummies.apply(lambda x: x * 1)

      t_step.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &lt;=250], df.wage[df.wage&lt;=250],marker = '*')
      plt.scatter(df.age[df.wage &gt;250], df.wage[df.wage&gt;250])
      plt.xlabel('Age')
      plt.ylabel('Wage')

      plt.plot(t_age, y_wage,color='red')
      plt.show()
    </program>
    <!-- horizontal rule in source notebook -->
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>For each of the functions <m>C_0(X)</m>, <m>C_1(X)</m>, <m>C_2(X)</m>, <m>C_3(X)</m>, <m>C_4(X)</m>, <m>C_5(X)</m> (following our notation in class), determine the domains where they have value 1.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Use the printed bin edges as <m>c_1,\ldots,c_5</m> and remember that <c>right=False</c> makes each finite interval left-closed and right-open.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><m>C_0</m> and <m>C_5</m> describe the two exterior intervals; <m>C_1</m> through <m>C_4</m> describe the four bins in the observed data range.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> The lower exterior interval is <m>(-\infty,c_1)</m>, not <m>(\infty,c_1)</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>With bin edges <m>18,33.5,49,64.5,80.062</m>, the indicator domains are: <m>C_0(X)=1</m> on <m>(-\infty,18)</m>; <m>C_1(X)=1</m> on <m>[18,33.5)</m>; <m>C_2(X)=1</m> on <m>[33.5,49)</m>; <m>C_3(X)=1</m> on <m>[49,64.5)</m>; <m>C_4(X)=1</m> on <m>[64.5,80.062)</m>; and <m>C_5(X)=1</m> on <m>[80.062,\infty)</m>.</p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Which of the functions <m>C_i(X)</m> for <m>i=0,\cdots, 5</m> have columns represented in this matrix? <em>Note: it's not all of them</em></p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Inspect <c>df_steps.columns</c> and compare its four intervals with the six conceptual indicator domains.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Only intervals containing observed ages are returned by this particular <c>pd.cut</c> call.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not count <m>C_0</m> and <m>C_5</m> merely because they exist in the mathematical definition; no observed value falls in those exterior intervals.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>It has <m>C_1, \cdots, C_4</m>. Basically the infinite interval ones <m>C_0</m> and <m>C_5</m> are dropped.</p>
              <p>The function I'm learning is</p>
              <p><md>
              f(X) = \beta_0 + \beta_1*C_1(X) + \beta_2 * C_2(X) + \beta_3*C_3(X) + \beta_4*C_4(X)
              </md></p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Pass this matrix to a linear regression model and use it to predict <c>wage</c>. What is the equation for your learned model?</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Fit <c>LinearRegression()</c> using <c>df_steps</c> as <m>X</m> and <c>df.wage</c> as <m>y</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Inspect both <c>coef_</c> and <c>intercept_</c>; each coefficient corresponds to one dummy column in <c>df_steps.columns</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Keep the dummy-column order when interpreting coefficients; otherwise a coefficient may be assigned to the wrong interval.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  linreg = LinearRegression()
                  linreg.fit(df_steps,df.wage)

                  print(linreg.coef_)
                  print(linreg.intercept_)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Be specific in terms of the <m>C_i</m> functions you learned earlier.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Write the prediction as an intercept plus one fitted coefficient times each represented <m>C_i(X)</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Use the column order from <c>df_steps</c> to map the four coefficients to <m>C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Because all four one-hot columns sum to one while an intercept is included, the coefficient representation is not unique; report the fitted scikit-learn parameterization consistently.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  coefs = [f"{beta:0.2e}" for beta in linreg.coef_]
                  eqstring = f"{linreg.intercept_:.2f}"

                  for i in range(len(coefs)):
                      eqstring += ' + ' + coefs[i] + ' C_' + str(i+1) + '(X)'

                  print(eqstring)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Above, I figured out the transformation of the input <c>t_age</c> values to do the same transformation as our step function. Now use the linear regression model learned to predict <c>y_wage</c>, then we can graph it.</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Call <c>linreg.predict</c> on the transformed step-function rows for <c>t_age</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Ensure the prediction matrix has the same dummy columns, in the same order, as the training matrix.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Do not recompute unrelated bin edges for the plotting grid; reuse the training <c>bins</c> so the learned step boundaries stay fixed.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  y_wage = linreg.predict(t_dummies)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
  </subsection>
